        <p class="numtag">01 / SIX</p>
        <p class="kick">The short answer</p>
        <h2>Infrared heats <i>you</i>. Stone heats <i>the room</i>.</h2>
      </div>
      <div class="rv">
        <p class="prose prose--lead" style="margin-bottom:32px;">
          That one line is the whole article. Everything below is just what it means when you are
          sitting in it.
        </p>
        <p class="prose">
          An infrared cabin uses electric panels to fire infrared waves straight at your body. The air
          around you stays fairly cool — usually 45 to 60°C. The heat lands on your skin, not in the
          space. It works much the same way a patio heater or a kebab grill works:
          <span class="mark">a directed beam of dry electric heat, pointed at you.</span>
        </p>
        <p class="prose">
          A volcanic stone sauna does the opposite. A Harvia heater brings a bed of stones up to
          temperature, and those stones heat the air in the cabin — typically 70 to 90°C. You are
          sitting <strong>inside</strong> the heat rather than in front of it. It surrounds you.
        </p>
      </div>
    </div>
  </div>
</section>

