        <p class="prose prose--dark">
          In a stone sauna you ladle water over the hot stones. It flashes into steam and rolls through
          the cabin in a wave. The Finns call this <strong>löyly</strong>. It is the whole point of the
          thing. Humidity spikes, the heat suddenly feels alive, and it is entirely under your hand.
          Want it hotter and heavier? Add water. Want to sit it out? Don't.
        </p>
        <p class="prose prose--dark">
          You cannot do this in an infrared cabin. There are no stones to pour onto. There is no steam.
          The heat is whatever the panel is set to, and that is that —
          <span class="mark">a fixed, dry, one-note burn on the skin.</span>
          Once you have felt the alternative, it is very hard to go back.
