        <p class="kick" style="color:rgba(255,255,255,0.85);">Why a barrel, specifically</p>
        <h2 class="light">The shape is <i style="color:var(--forest-ink);">doing work</i></h2>
      </div>
      <div class="rv">
        <p class="prose" style="color:rgba(255,255,255,0.94);">
          A barrel is not a styling decision. Heat rises and pools — in a square cabin it collects in
          the corners and sits there, dead, while your feet stay cold. A curved barrel has no corners
          to trap it. The air circulates continuously, so the heat is even from bench to floor, and the
          cabin comes up to temperature far faster.
        </p>
        <p class="prose" style="color:rgba(255,255,255,0.94);">
          Less air volume also means less power to run it. And the staved construction — the same
          principle as a whisky cask — expands and tightens as the timber takes on moisture. It seals
          itself. Which is why ours sit outside, through a Welsh winter, and keep going.
        </p>
      </div>
    </div>
  </div>

